WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.830
If you don’t have prior experience learning
foreign languages, then starting a new language

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:06.380
for the first time can be a really challenging
task.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.539
Even if you’re motivated, things aren’t
easy.

00:00:08.539 --> 00:00:13.089
At the beginning, lots of motivation and little
direction typically leads to a bookshelf full

00:00:13.089 --> 00:00:18.770
of unused language books, a phone full of
unopened apps, and a learning schedule that’s

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:23.360
all over the place—that is, if you spend
any time learning at all.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.420
Instead, most of your time is spent thinking.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.070
Or rather, worrying.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:30.410
You worry to yourself, asking questions like:

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:32.870
Which resource should start with?

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:33.910
When should I start?

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.290
How often should I sit down to learn?

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:39.680
How long should each of my learning sessions
be?

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.440
You’ve probably found most of these questions
hard to answer, especially if you don’t

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:45.940
have outside help.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:50.649
Where usually you could rely on a teacher
or tutor to provide solutions, learning independently

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:54.570
has left you with little to rely on other
than yourself.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.320
But it doesn’t have to be that way.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.430
With the right guide, and the right plan,
you can learn any language, even in the comfort

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:05.600
of your own home, far away from any language
classroom.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.880
Hello, I am Luca Lampariello, I am a language
coach who speaks 14 languages.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:15.720
On this channel, I help people learn foreign
languages more efficiently, faster, and in

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:16.720
a fun way.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.369
Today, I’d like to share three simple steps
that you can put to use today, to help you

00:01:22.369 --> 00:01:24.760
begin learning a new language—the smart
way!

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.160
Let’s dive right in!

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.030
Step 1: Get the Right Resources
When you’re just starting out in a language,

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:34.540
the most obvious thing to worry about is which
learning resources you will use.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.860
It doesn’t take more than a quick Google
search to reveal that for nearly any language

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:44.470
you want to learn, there are hundreds of books,
apps, courses, podcasts, flashcard decks,

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:47.680
and more, all intended to help you learn that
language.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.770
This might seem like a good thing, but the
sheer abundance of available learning materials

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:58.050
makes it impossible to navigate between them,
and choose which ones are actually ideal for

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.360
your own needs.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.470
So, despite the great number of resources,
most people get stuck at this stage, and struggle

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:05.590
to move forward.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:09.380
While there are many characteristics that
you could possibly look for in a good language

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.830
resource, I recommend that you focus on resources
that have two traits in particular.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.250
When choosing a language resource, aim for
materials that are:

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:21.380
Comprehensible (that is, they feature language
that is appropriate for your skill level)

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:25.880
Interesting (that is, they feature content
that you find engaging or compelling).

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:30.700
Resources that meet these two conditions are
most likely to keep you both challenged and

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:37.650
engaged for the longest period of time, which
will allow for the most learning to take place.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:41.620
In my experience, I’ve found that learning
materials with authentic, bilingual texts

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:46.540
in my target language are the ones that most
often meet the comprehensible and interesting

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:47.730
criteria.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:51.780
This goes for me, and the hundreds of language
learners I’ve coached personally.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:53.830
Why are bilingual texts so important?

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:58.900
Because having the text in both your target
language and native language (or a language

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:04.910
you know well) will help you compare and contrast
the structure of both languages.

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:09.660
This comparison will help you gradually gain
an understanding of how each language works

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.569
in relation to the other.

00:03:11.569 --> 00:03:16.099
This type of comparison is something that
you’ll do in your head (with or without

00:03:16.099 --> 00:03:21.680
a bilingual text), but having the texts in
front of you makes it a smoother, more painless

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:22.680
process.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.769
As a beginner, you’ll want to look for bilingual
texts that feature short, natural dialogues

00:03:26.769 --> 00:03:31.019
in your target language, along with audio
of those dialogues—even better if the dialogues

00:03:31.019 --> 00:03:36.470
also come with things like a vocabulary list
and simple grammar explanations.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:41.959
These extra tools will help quickly clarify
any words or grammar points that are difficult

00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:44.800
to grasp solely from examining both texts.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:50.010
There are a number of resources on the market
today that have all of the features I’ve

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:55.000
described, but I’m personally quite fond
of using Assimil’s “With Ease” series

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.010
for just this purpose.

00:03:57.010 --> 00:04:01.879
Assimil is a French company that provides
language coursebooks in a wide range of language

00:04:01.879 --> 00:04:02.879
combinations.

00:04:02.879 --> 00:04:06.560
These coursebooks are jam-packed with short,
funny dialogues that I’ve found enjoyable

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:08.640
across many different languages.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.500
If you can’t find Assimil in your language,
feel free to check the description box for

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:17.639
a link to my resources page, where you can
find many other beginner resources that I

00:04:17.639 --> 00:04:18.639
recommend.

00:04:18.639 --> 00:04:22.289
Step 2: Get Organized
Another important thing to keep in mind if

00:04:22.289 --> 00:04:27.669
you want to start learning a language successfully
is to get organized and stay organized from

00:04:27.669 --> 00:04:30.360
the very beginning and for a long time.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.379
Contrary to what you might think, language
learning is a marathon.

00:04:33.379 --> 00:04:38.770
It isn’t something that you can just do
for a few weeks or months, reach the level

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:40.610
you want, and then just forget about it.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:45.159
If you don’t use your language skills, you
lose them, so it’s of the utmost importance

00:04:45.159 --> 00:04:51.139
that before you start, you prepare yourself
to learn every day, over a long period of

00:04:51.139 --> 00:04:56.229
time, and the most important aspects of this
preparation are threefold:

00:04:56.229 --> 00:04:59.259
You must:
Create a learning space

00:04:59.259 --> 00:05:03.719
Organize a regular learning schedule
Cultivate a strong mindset

00:05:03.719 --> 00:05:09.520
For my learning space, I make sure to keep
a clean, organized, and distraction-free area

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:14.759
of my room where I can, you know, sit down
every day and learn languages.

00:05:14.759 --> 00:05:21.800
I learn in the same spot every day, so as
to train my brain that this is the spot where

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:22.800
learning happens.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:26.469
Your learning space can be anywhere you’d
like, however I’d recommend you try to keep

00:05:26.469 --> 00:05:31.309
it as free of distractions as possible, and
that you learn in that same location every

00:05:31.309 --> 00:05:32.309
time.

00:05:32.309 --> 00:05:35.710
For my learning schedule, I try to keep things
consistent and organized as well.

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:41.039
Specifically, I learn at the same time every
day, and almost always for the same amount

00:05:41.039 --> 00:05:42.039
of time.

00:05:42.039 --> 00:05:46.870
This helps make learning a habit.

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:51.820
For added benefit, I do this at the time of
day when I have the most energy, so that I

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:54.039
can focus better and more easily.

00:05:54.039 --> 00:06:00.069
For your learning schedule, aim for a daily
time slot of at least thirty to forty-five

00:06:00.069 --> 00:06:03.210
minutes, when you feel most awake and alert.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:08.719
Try not to skip it—if you do, definitely
don’t skip two days, as you risk breaking

00:06:08.719 --> 00:06:11.029
your learning habit altogether.

00:06:11.029 --> 00:06:15.490
For my mindset, I focus on discipline over
motivation.

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:16.909
Motivation is unreliable.

00:06:16.909 --> 00:06:21.939
Some days you’ve got enough to take on the
world, other days, it’s nowhere to be found.

00:06:21.939 --> 00:06:26.081
When embarking on a challenging, long-term
process like language learning, motivation

00:06:26.081 --> 00:06:31.550
usually ebbs and flows in unpredictable ways,
meaning you can rarely plan on having it when

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:32.550
you need it.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:35.909
Instead of motivation, I rely on discipline.

00:06:35.909 --> 00:06:40.789
Discipline is about showing up every day,
whether you “feel like it” or not.

00:06:40.789 --> 00:06:45.069
It’s about putting in the time, and putting
in the effort, even if you’re not quite

00:06:45.069 --> 00:06:47.800
seeing the results you expect.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:53.560
In a word, it’s about consistency, which,
if you noticed, is a core part of my learning

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.409
space, my learning schedule, and my mindset.

00:06:56.409 --> 00:07:00.770
If you’re feeling low on discipline, you
might be wondering how you can get some, too.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:04.080
Here’s a strategy I’ve often used that
you can try out yourself:

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.500
If you don’t have enough motivation to start
a difficult task (like language learning),

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:12.389
you can generate discipline by developing
a starting ritual.

00:07:12.389 --> 00:07:17.439
A starting ritual is a simple routine that
you can practice that trains your brain to

00:07:17.439 --> 00:07:20.360
think “ok, now it’s time to get started!”

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:26.789
Your starting ritual can be anything, but
I prefer short, meditative activities that

00:07:26.789 --> 00:07:27.840
I can do anywhere.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.270
In particular, before starting each of my
language learning sessions, I like to:

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:37.459
Do one minute of breathing exercises
Do one minute of walking around the room

00:07:37.459 --> 00:07:42.469
And sit down at my learning space
Once I’ve done my breathing and walking

00:07:42.469 --> 00:07:46.849
exercises, then I know that sitting down is
the signal to get started learning.

00:07:46.849 --> 00:07:52.749
It sounds simple—and it is!—but these
kinds of starting rituals can be quite effective

00:07:52.749 --> 00:07:55.819
at getting started, even when motivation is
low.

00:07:55.819 --> 00:08:00.669
If you build the habit of starting even when
you don’t feel like it, then you’re cultivating

00:08:00.669 --> 00:08:05.689
discipline, which will be an invaluable tool
along your learning journey.

00:08:05.689 --> 00:08:10.500
Step 3: Reflect on Your Progress Every Day
As I said before, learning a language is a

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:13.110
process of long-term growth.

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:17.520
Though you are learning every day, the steady
progress you are making can be difficult—or

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.039
impossible—to see.

00:08:19.039 --> 00:08:24.659
If you can’t see your progress, it can be
easy to become demotivated.

00:08:24.659 --> 00:08:29.199
And though I said earlier that I don’t rely
on motivation, I do believe it’s important

00:08:29.199 --> 00:08:31.439
to watch out for demotivation.

00:08:31.439 --> 00:08:36.110
If left unchecked, demotivation often causes
people to give up, and stop learning altogether.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:41.050
To avoid the trap of demotivation, it’s
important to make your progress visible, and

00:08:41.050 --> 00:08:43.000
to review it regularly.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:46.089
I do this through keeping a language learning
notebook.

00:08:46.089 --> 00:08:52.139
Now, I say “notebook”, but what I really
mean is “a space to write about your language

00:08:52.139 --> 00:08:53.139
learning activities”.

00:08:53.139 --> 00:08:56.029
It doesn’t have to be a physical notebook,
or journal.

00:08:56.029 --> 00:08:59.110
In fact, I often use Google Docs for just
this purpose.

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:03.569
Every day, in my language learning notebook,
I write down the language learning activities

00:09:03.569 --> 00:09:07.649
that I have done for that day. and these are
usually listening, reading, and translation

00:09:07.649 --> 00:09:12.750
activities tied to my Bidirectional Translation
Method, which is what I personally use to

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:13.759
start any language.

00:09:13.759 --> 00:09:17.800
Beyond the list of activities, I also ask
myself questions which help me reflect on

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:20.920
the quality of my learning for that day.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:25.170
These include questions such as:
Do I enjoy my current learning activities?

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:30.329
Do I find them too easy, too difficult, or
just challenging enough?

00:09:30.329 --> 00:09:34.529
Can I add or remove anything to make these
activities more effective?

00:09:34.529 --> 00:09:38.399
Is there anything I can do to better adapt
these activities to the language I am learning?

00:09:38.399 --> 00:09:43.190
Asking myself these questions keeps me from
getting complacent in my learning activities,

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:48.360
and helps me to strive to improve them more
and more over time.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:53.209
Reflecting in this way helps me not only keep
track of my developing language skills, but

00:09:53.209 --> 00:09:58.399
also my developing strategies and techniques
for learning languages, as well.

00:09:58.399 --> 00:10:00.350
Ok, so there you have it.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:05.410
Now you’ve learned the three simple steps
that you can put to use today to start learning

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:06.410
any language.

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:10.870
If you’re interested in learning more about
how I start learning new languages on my own,

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:12.839
check the link in the description box.

00:10:12.839 --> 00:10:18.519
You’ll find a link to my brand-new online
course, which teaches the ins and outs of

00:10:18.519 --> 00:10:21.879
my very own Bidirectional Translation method!

00:10:21.879 --> 00:10:23.649
As always, thanks for watching and happy language
learning!

